    "compose_patient": {
      "sections": [
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "You came in while in labour at 39 weeks. Your baby's heart rate monitor showed signs of distress. Because of this, you needed an emergency caesarean section (LSCS).\n\nAfter the birth, you had heavy bleeding. This is called a postpartum haemorrhage (PPH). It happened because the womb did not contract as it should (uterine atony)."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "Your baby boy was delivered safely by emergency caesarean section.\n\nTo treat the heavy bleeding, the team used:\n- medicines to help the womb contract (oxytocin and ergometrine)\n- a technique called bimanual compression\n\nThe bleeding was brought under control. You did not need to go back to theatre.\n\nA blood test on day 1 after the operation showed your blood count had fallen. Because of this, iron tablets were started.\n\nYou were also given a daily injection to thin the blood (enoxaparin). This was to reduce the risk of a blood clot, which can be higher after a caesarean section and heavy bleeding."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7",
            "sections.8"
          ],
          "text": "- A community midwife handover has been arranged.\n- Your GP and health visitor have been informed.\n- You have a postnatal check with your GP at 6 weeks."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.4",
